// error
// #4487: `Enum.Variant(args)` was judged on payload ARITY alone
// (compiler/validatevariant.bit's `vVariantArity`, then in validateexpr.bit).
// Nothing type-checked a payload argument, so any expression whose COUNT
// matched was lowered as if it already had the declared payload type: `E.F(a)`
// for `a: f32` into an `f64` payload built rc=0 and printed the f32 bit
// pattern read as an f64 (`F 0.0000...5222099017`), and `E.I(b)` for
// `b: bool` into an `i64` payload built rc=0 and printed `I 1`. Silent wrong
// answers, which this repo ranks worse than a crash.
//
// Each payload argument now goes through `vExpectExpr`, the same call every
// other argument position uses, so the diagnostic is the `let` path's E0041 at
// the ARGUMENT's own span — not the whole-call span the E0050 arity
// diagnostic uses. The third case pins that the arity rule still fires alone:
// under a wrong count the type errors are suppressed, so one diagnostic
// explains the call instead of N.
//
// The fourth case is `nil` into a CLASS payload. On main that was accepted
// while every other position rejected it -- `let a: Circle = nil`,
// `take(nil)` and `Outer{ i: nil }` are all E0041 there -- so the payload was
// the one hole, and closing it is the uniformity this change buys rather than
// a new rule. `nil` into an INTERFACE payload stays legal and is pinned in the
// ACCEPT fixture.
//
// The ACCEPT half lives in _tests_/cases/run_variant_payload_ok.bit: untyped
// constant widening, a slice-literal payload, an interface payload, `nil` into
// one, a nested enum and a generic payload must all still compile.
class Circle {
  r: f64,
}

enum E {
  F(f64),
  I(i64),
  C(Circle),
}

fn takesE(e: E): i64 {
  match (e) {
    F(x) => return i64(x)
    I(x) => return x
    C(c) => return i64(c.r)
  }
}

fn main() {
  let a: f32 = 0.5
  let b: bool = true
  println("${takesE(E.F(a))}")
  println("${takesE(E.I(b))}")
  println("${takesE(E.C(nil))}")
  println("${takesE(E.F(a, a))}")
}
